    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0256",
      "sequence": 268,
      "meaning": "Sītā’s mother asks why all who have come to watch, though called their elders and well-wishers, do not explain to the guru that such insistence is unsuitable for a boy. Rāvaṇa and Bāṇa could not even touch the bow, and every boastful king has failed; why place it in a young prince’s hands? Could a young swan lift Mount Mandara? A clever friend answers gently that everyone’s wisdom has run out and fate’s course cannot be understood, but power must not be judged small by size. How far apart in size are pitcher-born Agastya and the boundless ocean? Yet his glory for drinking it fills the world. The sun’s disk looks small, but at its rising the three worlds’ darkness flees. A supremely short mantra controls Brahmā, Hari, Śiva and all the gods; a tiny goad masters a greatly intoxicated elephant king.",
