    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0257",
      "sequence": 269,
      "meaning": "Kāma took flower-arrows and a flower-bow yet brought all the worlds under his sway. Therefore, O queen, abandon doubt: Rāma will break the bow. Trust arises from the friend’s words; sorrow recedes and love greatly increases. Looking toward Rāma with a fearful heart, Vaidehī silently petitions one deity after another. Agitated, she prays inwardly: be gracious, Maheśa and Bhavānī; make my service fruitful, show favour and remove the bow’s heaviness. O Gaṇeśa, leader of hosts and giver of boons, I have served you until today; hear my repeated plea and make the bow’s weight very slight. Gazing again and again at Raghubīra, she steadies herself and invokes the gods, while her eyes fill with loving tears and rows of gooseflesh cover her body.",
