    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0258",
      "sequence": 270,
      "meaning": "After drinking in Rāma’s beauty with full eyes, Sītā remembers her father’s vow and her mind is shaken again: alas, Father has undertaken a cruel resolve without understanding gain or loss. His frightened ministers offer no counsel, and the assembly of wise men permits a great wrong. How distant are the two—the bow, harder even than a thunderbolt, and this dark, tender-bodied youth! How can she keep courage? Would one pierce a diamond with a śirīṣa flower? She turns inwardly to Śiva’s bow: the whole assembly’s judgment has grown confused, so now cast your own rigidity upon these people and become light upon seeing Raghupati. In Sītā’s intense anguish, each tiny moment passes like an age. She gazes at the Lord, then at the ground; her restless eyes seem like a pair of Love’s fish playing in a swaying moon-orb.",
