    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0260",
      "sequence": 272,
      "meaning": "Lakṣmaṇa commands the elephants of the quarters, the cosmic tortoise, serpent and boar to hold the earth firmly and not let it move: Rāma wishes to break Śaṅkara’s bow, so all must be alert at his order. When Rāma approaches the bow, men, women and gods invoke their accumulated merit. Everyone’s doubt and ignorance, the dull kings’ pride, Paraśurāma’s weighty arrogance, the gods’ and great sages’ timidity, Sītā’s worry, Janaka’s regret and the queens’ forest-fire of pain all board the great boat of Śiva’s bow together. Before the boundless ocean of Rāma’s arm-strength, however, none can serve as helmsman to carry that freight across. Everyone stares at Rāma like figures in a painting; Sītā gazes upon the abode of compassion in exceptional distress.",
